<rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"><channel><language>en</language><title>Breaking news from AFRICA, Maghreb and Sub-Saharan Africa - France 24</title><description>FRANCE 24’s coverage of events, politics and social issues across Africa, including North Africa (the Maghreb) and the Sahel, aided by our network of correspondents and experts across the continent.</description><link>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/</link><lastBuildDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 05:05:31 GMT</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/rss" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><category>Africa</category><title>DR Congo’s former president Kabila to lead new movement with M23 rebels</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20261012-dr-congo-former-president-joseph-kabila-to-lead-new-movement-with-m23-rebels</link><description>
 
 
Former DR Congo president Joseph Kabila announced a new political-military alliance with Rwanda-backed M23 rebels on Monday, despite being sentenced to death in absentia for alleged complicity with the group. The move comes as debate grows over constitutional changes ahead of President Felix Tshisekedi’s final term ending in 2028.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/7910c4e6-7f90-11f0-99ca-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/000-48NY9FH.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/7910c4e6-7f90-11f0-99ca-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/000-48NY9FH.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">c1a5a36c-c683-11f1-bec4-a1ea1072ad80</guid><pubDate>Mon, 12 Oct 2026 21:27:41 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/7910c4e6-7f90-11f0-99ca-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/000-48NY9FH.jpg">© Jospin Mwisha, AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>Ethiopian govt forces enter key Tigray town near Eritrea border</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/ethiopian-govt-forces-enter-key-tigray-town-near-eritrea-border</link><description>
In tonight's edition, Ethiopian forces tighten their grip on Tigray, advancing into the key town of Adwa after rebels withdrew. Also, police arrested 30 people amid violence across South Africa. And we head to Tunisia, where ancient Sufi chants collide with pulsating electronic beats! 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a726f12e-c67b-11f1-8fe9-3774aeac812a/w:1024/p:16x9/DN197525-A-01-20261007-01.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a726f12e-c67b-11f1-8fe9-3774aeac812a/w:1024/p:16x9/DN197525-A-01-20261007-01.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">0368a950-c67c-11f1-b90f-e322dce06a8f</guid><pubDate>Mon, 12 Oct 2026 20:34:04 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Jean-Emile JAMMINE</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>At least 139 migrants die in boat wreck between Djibouti and Yemen</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/at-least-139-migrants-die-in-boat-wreck-between-djibouti-and-yemen</link><description>
At least 139 migrants died, including 15 children and 84 went missing when an overcrowded boat sank on its way from Djibouti to Yemen on the night between October 8 and 9. The International Organization for Migration said most of the people were Ethiopian when the vessel capsized off north of the town of Obock. Details by Morgan Ayre.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/f1dcbd28-c623-11f1-80e9-77317c09f325/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261012-083759-083927-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/f1dcbd28-c623-11f1-80e9-77317c09f325/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261012-083759-083927-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">f6bb8202-c623-11f1-91f2-e9bc5d89a6fc</guid><pubDate>Mon, 12 Oct 2026 10:24:11 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Scores of gold miners killed in air strikes on Egypt-Sudan border, rights group says</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20261012-egypt-sudan-border-airstrikes-gold-miners-killed</link><description>
At least 72 workers were killed in air strikes on a gold mine on the Sudan-Egypt border on Saturday, a Sudanese rights group said on Sunday. The Emergency Lawyers organisation accused Egypt’s air force of the attack. AFP could not independently verify the account and Egypt has issued no official comment.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/ed29f7ca-c5db-11f1-8dcf-29a2e29ae696/w:1024/p:16x9/AP16118579529500.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/ed29f7ca-c5db-11f1-8dcf-29a2e29ae696/w:1024/p:16x9/AP16118579529500.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">32cf1d00-c5dc-11f1-91f7-9d438446f071</guid><pubDate>Mon, 12 Oct 2026 01:28:27 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/ed29f7ca-c5db-11f1-8dcf-29a2e29ae696/w:1024/p:16x9/AP16118579529500.jpg">© Brian Rohan, AP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>At least 139 migrants dead, more than 84 missing after boat capsizes off Djibouti</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20261011-at-least-hundred-migrants-dead-scores-missing-after-boat-capsizes-off-djibouti</link><description>
At least 139 migrants, including at least 15 children, died and 84 remain missing after a boat carrying 350 people capsized off Djibouti en route to Yemen, the International Organisation for Migration said on Sunday.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/b04f8942-c57b-11f1-9bf6-53993c5f1f18/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D2WH3JN.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/b04f8942-c57b-11f1-9bf6-53993c5f1f18/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D2WH3JN.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">c0963284-c552-11f1-be29-d3f466d13013</guid><pubDate>Sun, 11 Oct 2026 09:24:40 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/b04f8942-c57b-11f1-9bf6-53993c5f1f18/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D2WH3JN.jpg">© Organisation internationale pour les migrations (OIM) de Djibouti via AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>South African judge Navi Pillay awarded Nobel Peace Prize</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/south-african-judge-awarded-nobel-peace-prize</link><description>
In tonight's programme: South African judge and human rights champion Navi Pillay has been awarded the Nobel Peace Prize. Also, in Kenya, health officials in Nairobi are still racing to track dozens of people linked to the man who died from Ebola earlier this week. Finally, Moroccan-British photographer Hassan Hajjaj has built a global reputation with his vibrant portraits, and 40 of his iconic "Rock Stars" are currently taking centre stage in Paris.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a324549c-c42b-11f1-aa1d-53993c5f1f18/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-5919164626ac961f82872c4-40513973.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a324549c-c42b-11f1-aa1d-53993c5f1f18/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-5919164626ac961f82872c4-40513973.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">bc0dd902-c42a-11f1-875a-b5bde722ec7a</guid><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 21:53:23 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Clarisse FORTUNÉ</dc:creator></item><item><category>Revisited</category><title>Sierra Leone: From 'blood diamonds' to stones of hope?</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/revisited/20261009-sierra-leone-from-blood-diamonds-to-stones-of-hopen</link><description>
"Blood diamonds" became infamous for funding the brutal civil war in Sierra Leone. Beginning 25 years ago, the Kimberley Process aimed to put an end to this illegal trade. Accused of complicity in the conflict, the diamond industry has since sought to clean up its image, emphasising an ethical shift. But a quarter of a century on, have the former "blood diamonds" truly become synonymous with development? FRANCE 24's Sarah Sakho, Simon Martin and Saidu Bah report.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/76fec852-c30e-11f1-a6dc-c55799ef8309/w:1024/p:16x9/billet-retour-diamant-EN.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/76fec852-c30e-11f1-a6dc-c55799ef8309/w:1024/p:16x9/billet-retour-diamant-EN.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">562b8438-c266-11f1-b9ff-d73c73a526c3</guid><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 13:58:26 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Simon MARTIN</dc:creator></item><item><category>The 51%</category><title>Foreign aid to Africa falls by over 25%: How women and children are bearing the brunt</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-51/20261009-foreign-aid-to-africa-falls-by-over-25-how-women-and-children-are-bearing-the-brunt</link><description>
As international aid to Africa dries up, what does it mean for women and children on the ground? We report from Kenya on the impact of cuts to US-aid backed health programmes for women. Annette Young also talks to Nana Zulu from the African Women's Development Fund on what it means for the region as a whole. Plus the bravery of Borneo's female firefighters; as Indonesia endures its worst fire season in 11 years, these women are also facing a sexist backlash.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/465d149a-c3c4-11f1-803c-27c2853ad5a5/w:1024/p:16x9/7d98784e-a8c4-4afc-a8dd-586f8be894cc.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/465d149a-c3c4-11f1-803c-27c2853ad5a5/w:1024/p:16x9/7d98784e-a8c4-4afc-a8dd-586f8be894cc.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">704cbb5e-c32c-11f1-b9af-b9879210c46e</guid><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 12:22:30 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Annette YOUNG</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Eritrea and Ethiopia accuse each other of provoking war as Tigray rebels retreat</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20261009-eritrea-ethiopia-accuse-each-other-of-provoking-war-as-tigray-rebels-retreat</link><description>
Ethiopia and Eritrea on Friday accused each other of starting a "war of agresson" days after the two countries severed diplomatic ties. Ethiopian officals estimate that up to 8,000 Eritrean soldiers are present in Tigray supporting rebels who last month launched attacks against federal forces.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/bc716724-c3d5-11f1-8b99-27c2853ad5a5/w:1024/p:16x9/278c35df4d8cae238940d8d675a1af914b2ba2a4.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/bc716724-c3d5-11f1-8b99-27c2853ad5a5/w:1024/p:16x9/278c35df4d8cae238940d8d675a1af914b2ba2a4.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">bd0756d0-c3d5-11f1-b959-09f43b7eeaf1</guid><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 11:36:58 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/bc716724-c3d5-11f1-8b99-27c2853ad5a5/w:1024/p:16x9/278c35df4d8cae238940d8d675a1af914b2ba2a4.jpg">© AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>Court of Arbitration for Sport hears Senegal's appeal over AFCON title</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/eye-on-africa/20261009-court-of-arbitration-for-sport-hears-senegal-s-appeal-over-afcon-title</link><description>
Ten months after the explosive AFCON final, Senegal and Morocco are still fighting over the title, this time at sport's highest court. A verdict on Senegal's appeal against Morocco's victory by default is expected within days. Also, it's a race against time in Kenya. Health officials are tracing dozens of contacts after an Ebola patient died following a journey from DR Congo. Plus Zimbabwean author Simbarashe Steyn Kundizeza wins the 2026 Caine Prize for African Writing for his short story "The Blue Fighter".
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/35d97678-c3c8-11f1-a607-ef6152f87623/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-8777045926ac8bb2893a9c7-47202247.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/35d97678-c3c8-11f1-a607-ef6152f87623/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-8777045926ac8bb2893a9c7-47202247.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">f733c5b8-c35e-11f1-826e-6b4421e397a2</guid><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 10:48:41 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Clarisse FORTUNÉ</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Senegal and Morocco set for AFCON title rematch – in Swiss sports court</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20261008-senegal-and-morocco-set-for-afcon-title-rematch-%E2%80%93-swiss-sports-court</link><description>
Senegal and Morocco on Thursday are again set to fight for the African Cup of Nations title – this time, in the Court of Arbitration for Sport in Switzerland. The closed-door hearing comes nine months after the Confederation of African Football stripped Senegal of their trophy after the team's players walked off the pitch to protest a penalty during the chaotic final roughly nine months ago. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/9d2e57b8-b87d-11f1-859e-852b23529bb9/w:1024/p:16x9/J24-CAN.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/9d2e57b8-b87d-11f1-859e-852b23529bb9/w:1024/p:16x9/J24-CAN.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">4bbaae9e-c2dd-11f1-8ea9-0df6d07fe57d</guid><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 07:17:51 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/9d2e57b8-b87d-11f1-859e-852b23529bb9/w:1024/p:16x9/J24-CAN.jpg">© Franck Fife, AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>Africa joins the data centre backlash</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/africa-joins-the-data-center-backlash</link><description>
In tonight's edition: Witnesses say Eritrean troops have moved into Ethiopia's Tigray region, but Asmara rejects the reports. Also, the African Union launches the Africa Credit Rating Agency, aiming to assess the financial health of African states and companies. Plus South African civil society is pushing back against the expansion of data centres that require vast amounts of electricity, water and land. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/179bb660-c28f-11f1-98b3-4b195755b8ed/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-15022793656ac6add5610765-35279800.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/179bb660-c28f-11f1-98b3-4b195755b8ed/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-15022793656ac6add5610765-35279800.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">0120c1b6-c28d-11f1-a78a-233673334bc7</guid><pubDate>Wed, 07 Oct 2026 20:37:55 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Clarisse FORTUNÉ</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>Kenya reports its first-ever Ebola death</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/kenya-reports-first-ever-ebola-death</link><description>
In tonight's edition: Kenya records its first Ebola death after a man travelled there from the DR Congo via Uganda. Also, Comoros is tightening its rules on who can do business. And in South Africa, concerns are growing that xenophobia and hostility toward foreign nationals could be driving African tourists away.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/c48e4248-c1cc-11f1-891c-0f96b136895a/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-12432674096ac567cfa9d0e5-47237078.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/c48e4248-c1cc-11f1-891c-0f96b136895a/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-12432674096ac567cfa9d0e5-47237078.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">5cdfa51a-c1cc-11f1-bdbf-993d13194231</guid><pubDate>Tue, 06 Oct 2026 21:28:01 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Clarisse FORTUNÉ</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Kenya reports first-ever Ebola death after patient returns from DR Congo</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20261006-kenya-reports-first-ever-ebola-death-after-patient-returns-from-dr-congo</link><description>
Kenya on Tuesday reported its first-ever Ebola death after a Kenyan national who had been living in the Democratic Republic of Congo (DR Congo) died after returning to the country. Kenya is the fourth country to record an Ebola case in the current outbreak, which was declared in eastern DR Congo in mid-May.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/9f9c5870-c196-11f1-8894-0f96b136895a/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26279506107815.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/9f9c5870-c196-11f1-8894-0f96b136895a/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26279506107815.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">29cacdc4-c197-11f1-9d53-79f422015548</guid><pubDate>Tue, 06 Oct 2026 15:41:44 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/9f9c5870-c196-11f1-8894-0f96b136895a/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26279506107815.jpg">© Andrew Kasuku, AP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>Ethiopia again demands access to the Red Sea</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/ethiopia-again-demands-access-to-the-red-sea</link><description>
In tonight's edition: Ethiopia's leader Abiy Ahmed secures another term in office, but his country is sliding back into conflict. Also, several major cities across Senegal are battered by torrential rains. Plus some of the migrants being deported from the US fear being sent to African countries criminalising homosexuality. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/519316da-c161-11f1-84a2-13e372f41e70/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-21255440696ac4b38a8fd698-98071912.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/519316da-c161-11f1-84a2-13e372f41e70/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-21255440696ac4b38a8fd698-98071912.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">12238914-c0fb-11f1-8f14-d71af4b81a74</guid><pubDate>Mon, 05 Oct 2026 20:28:19 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Clarisse FORTUNÉ</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Rwandan genocide suspect 'hunter' Dafroza Gauthier dies at 72</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20261004-rwandan-genocide-hunter-dafroza-gauthier-dies-at-72</link><description>
For nearly 30 years, Dafroza Gauthier, along with her husband Alain, tracked down Rwandan refugees in France suspected of having participated in the Tutsi genocide. She died in Reims on Saturday after dedicating her life to bringing to justice those allegedly responsible for the 1994 massacre of more than 800,000 people. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/59ce40b6-bfd8-11f1-82be-1ff0321dbbd3/w:1024/p:16x9/AP23327396671319.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/59ce40b6-bfd8-11f1-82be-1ff0321dbbd3/w:1024/p:16x9/AP23327396671319.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">a37d0da2-bfea-11f1-8923-bb89061cdae9</guid><pubDate>Sun, 04 Oct 2026 12:12:24 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/59ce40b6-bfd8-11f1-82be-1ff0321dbbd3/w:1024/p:16x9/AP23327396671319.jpg">© Christophe Ena, AP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Ethiopian federal forces control Tigray capital as regional rivals meet in Egypt</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20261004-ethiopian-federal-forces-control-tigray-capital-as-regional-rivals-meet-in-egypt</link><description>
Troops loyal to Ethiopia's federal government were patrolling the streets of Mekelle on Sunday, a day after the Tigray People's Liberation Front (TPLF) withdrew its forces from the northern city. The government advance came as Ethiopia's regional rivals including Eritrea and Sudan met in the Egyptian coastal city of El-Alamein, calling for regional disputes to be settled through "peaceful means".
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/7a003590-c00c-11f1-a8ec-dd51fcab6130/w:1024/p:16x9/e214db71697af78d2ba3e67885af1f946ef58644.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/7a003590-c00c-11f1-a8ec-dd51fcab6130/w:1024/p:16x9/e214db71697af78d2ba3e67885af1f946ef58644.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">89c12478-bfdd-11f1-9766-37c6a4d39a45</guid><pubDate>Sun, 04 Oct 2026 10:22:43 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/7a003590-c00c-11f1-a8ec-dd51fcab6130/w:1024/p:16x9/e214db71697af78d2ba3e67885af1f946ef58644.jpg">© AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Ethiopia's Tigray rebels abandon regional capital Mekelle as government forces enter city</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20261003-ethiopia-s-tigray-rebels-abandon-regional-capital-of-mekelle-as-pro-government-forces-advance</link><description>
Ethiopian federal forces have entered the capital of the northern Tigray region and seized its airport, witnesses said Saturday, reversing a takeover by rebels from the Tigray People's Liberation Front (TPLF) that prompted the suspension of commercial flights last month. The TPLF has been fighting against pro-government forces since September 23, raising fears of an all-out civil war similar to that of 2020-2022.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/c5c37128-bf07-11f1-ba42-1ff0321dbbd3/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-09-24T101038Z-2032759567-RC2KPNA9407F-RTRMADP-3-ETHIOPIA-SECURITY.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/c5c37128-bf07-11f1-ba42-1ff0321dbbd3/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-09-24T101038Z-2032759567-RC2KPNA9407F-RTRMADP-3-ETHIOPIA-SECURITY.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">42404cba-bf00-11f1-9a09-4fc00ac8b5ad</guid><pubDate>Sat, 03 Oct 2026 07:58:45 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/c5c37128-bf07-11f1-ba42-1ff0321dbbd3/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-09-24T101038Z-2032759567-RC2KPNA9407F-RTRMADP-3-ETHIOPIA-SECURITY.jpg">© Reuters (File)</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>Ethiopian army pushes back Tigrayan rebels</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/eye-on-africa/20261002-ethiopian-army-pushes-back-tigrayan-rebels</link><description>
